    [168, "hmmt_feb2025", "Let $f$ be the unique polynomial of degree at most $2026$ such that for all $n \\in\\{1,2,3, \\ldots, 2027\\}$,\n$$\nf(n)= \\begin{cases}1 & \\text { if } n \\text { is a perfect square } \\\\ 0 & \\text { otherwise }\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $\\frac{a}{b}$ is the coefficient of $x^{2025}$ in $f$, where $a$ and $b$ are integers such that $\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b)=1$. Compute the unique integer $r$ between $0$ and $2026$…", "1037", [19428, 0, 0, "0", " \\(p = 5\\)), we found the coefficient of \\(x^{p-2}\\) to be zero modulo \\(p\\).\n   - This suggests a general result where the coefficient of \\(x^{2025}\\) in \\(f(x)\\) modulo 2027 is zero, leading to \\(r \\equiv 0 \\mod 2027\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nAfter detailed analysis and testing specific cases, the coefficient of \\(x^{2025}\\) modulo 2027 is found to be zero. Thus, the integer \\(r\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]"], [13492, 0, 0, "1", "(2027\\).\n   - Since \\(a = 1\\) and \\(b = 1\\), the equation becomes:\n     \\[\n     1 - r \\cdot 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2027}.\n     \\]\n   - Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     1 - r \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2027}.\n     \\]\n   - Therefore:\n     \\[\n     r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2027}.\n     \\]\n   - Since \\(r\\) must be an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(2026\\), the unique solution is \\(r = 1\\).\n\nThus, the integer \\(r\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\)."]],
